We can use require(Boolean expression) to evaluate the arguments passed to a method which will throw
java.lang.IllegalArgumentException if the arguments doesn't satisfy the condition we passed.


def isValidNumber(s: String): Boolean = {
    @tailrec
    def allDigits(i: Int): Boolean =
      if (i >= s.length) true
      else if (s(i) >= '0' && s(i) <= '9') allDigits(i + 1)
      else false

    val start = if (s.startsWith("+") || s.startsWith("-")) 1 else 0
    if (s.length <= start) false
    else allDigits(start)
}

val raw = List("12", "abc", "-45", "", "7", "3.5", "+9")
val parseWhole : PartialFunction[String, Int] = { case n if isValidNumber(n) => n.toInt }

raw.map(parseWhole) -- map method demands a total function it applies to every element in the collection and the first
element outside the domain blows up and causes MatchError

raw.collect(parseWhole) -- on the other hand collect asks the PartialFunction if it is defined for this input,
if it is true applies the function else silently drops it. Filtering and traversal in one line

raw.flatMap(_.toIntOption) -- this does the same thing as collect with PartialFunction we wrote
    toIntOption returns None if it is not an Int and flatMap silently drops it

